<header><p class="enc-kicker">State</p><h1 class="enc-title">West Pakistan</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1955">1955</a>–<a href="/years/1970">1970</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">West <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pakistan">Pakistan</a> was the single province formed in 1955 under the One Unit scheme by merging the provinces and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/princely-state">princely states</a> of the western wing of Pakistan, including <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/punjab">Punjab</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sindh">Sindh</a>, the North-West Frontier, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/balochistan">Baluchistan</a>, Bahawalpur, Khairpur and the Baluchistan States Union. The princely states lost their separate status. It was dissolved in 1970 and the old provinces were restored.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/west-pakistan.jpg" alt="West Pakistan" width="320" height="320" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: EnchantedEdits · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:West_Pakistan_(orthographic_projection).png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>At independence in 1947 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pakistan">Pakistan</a> comprised two regions separated by about 1,600 km of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a>. The western wing was made up of three governor's provinces, the North-West Frontier, West Punjab and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sind">Sind</a>, together with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/balochistan">Baluchistan</a>, the Baluchistan States Union, several <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/princely-state">princely states</a> including Bahawalpur, Chitral, Dir, Hunza, Khairpur and Swat, the Karachi Federal Capital Territory and the tribal areas. The eastern wing was the single province of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/east-bengal">East Bengal</a>. The partition divided resources unequally: Pakistan received 22 percent of the land, 9 percent of the population and 36 percent of the military forces, and its facilities were mostly obsolete, with a week's ammunition reserve.</p><p>The western wing dominated politics, although <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/east-pakistan">East Pakistan</a> held more than half of the population and a disproportionately small number of seats in the Constituent Assembly. This inequality and the distance between the wings were believed to be delaying a constitution. The government therefore announced the One Unit policy on 22 November 1954, merging the western provinces, states and tribal areas into one province to mirror the single province in the east.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>The province was composed of twelve divisions. Its capital was first <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/karachi">Karachi</a>, later <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lahore">Lahore</a>, and in 1965 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/islamabad">Islamabad</a>. The constitution of 1956 confirmed a parliamentary form of government, with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/islam">Islam</a> as the state religion and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/urdu">Urdu</a>, English and Bengali as state languages. Violence in Lahore from 1953 led to martial law there. From 1947 to 1959 the government was only partly stable, with seven prime ministers removed. A military coup in 1958 brought General <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ayub-khan">Ayub Khan</a> to power, and the capital moved to Rawalpindi in 1959.</p><p>The One Unit programme met harsh opposition and civil unrest, and the four provinces struggled for its abolition. A committee for its dissolution worked with the government of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yahya-khan">Yahya Khan</a>. West <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pakistan">Pakistan</a> was abolished on 1 July 1970 under the Legal Framework Order, and the provinces of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/balochistan">Balochistan</a>, the North-West Frontier, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/punjab">Punjab</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sindh">Sindh</a> were restored.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Legacy</h2><p>In the general elections of December 1970 the Bengali Awami League under Mujibur Rahman won all but two of the 162 seats allocated to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/east-pakistan">East Pakistan</a>. The military government did not allow him to form a government. A war followed between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pakistan">Pakistan</a> and Bengali nationalists, the Mukti Bahini, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a> intervened in support of them. East Pakistan became the independent state of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bangladesh">Bangladesh</a> on 16 December 1971, and the term West Pakistan became redundant. The 1961 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/census">census</a> counted 42.8 million people in West Pakistan, about 45 percent of the 93 million in the whole country.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “West Pakistan” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>In the Punjab riots between 200,000 and 2,000,000 people were killed; some 6.5 million Muslims moved to <b>West Pakistan</b> and 4.7 million Hindus and Sikhs to India, the largest migration in human history, and a dispute over Jammu and Kashmir brought war with India in 1947–1948.</blockquote><a href="/empire/islamic-republic-of-pakistan">Islamic Republic of Pakistan · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>At the Ramna Race Course, before over one million people and amid escalating tensions with <b>West Pakistan</b>, Mujib informally declared the independence of Bangladesh: &quot;The struggle this time, is a struggle for our liberty.&quot;</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/march-7">7 March in history</a></li><li><blockquote>Bahawalpur was merged into <b>West Pakistan</b> in 1955 and is now a division of Punjab province.</blockquote><a href="/state/bahawalpur-state">Bahawalpur State</a></li><li><blockquote>In 1955 the union was dissolved and merged into <b>West Pakistan</b> under the One Unit scheme, ending the political role of the Baloch rulers.</blockquote><a href="/state/baluchistan-states-union">Baluchistan States Union</a></li><li><blockquote>On 14 August 1947 the Dominion of Pakistan came into being, with Muhammad Ali Jinnah as Governor-General and Karachi as capital, comprising <b>West Pakistan</b> and East Bengal.</blockquote><a href="/state/dominion-of-pakistan">Dominion of Pakistan</a></li><li><blockquote>Khairpur was merged into <b>West Pakistan</b> in 1955 and is now a district of Sindh.</blockquote><a href="/state/khairpur-state">Khairpur State</a></li></ul></section>
